### 🛠️ The "Safety Net" (Rescue Commands)
If things go sideways and you land in a `grub>` or `grub rescue>` prompt, don't panic. Here is your manual recovery manual:

#### If you are in `grub rescue>` (The "Basic" Prompt)
This usually means GRUB can't find its filesystem modules.
```bash
# 1. List partitions to find Parrot
ls

# 2. Set the prefix and root (Assuming hd0,nvme0n1p3 or wherever Parrot lives)
set prefix=(hd0,gpt3)/boot/grub
set root=(hd0,gpt3)

# 3. Load the normal module
insmod normal
normal
```

#### If you are in `grub>` (The "Normal" Prompt)
If you see the menu but Parrot won't boot, or you need to manually trigger the wake-up:
```bash
# 1. Manually load the kernel with the resume flag
linux /boot/vmlinuz-6.10.11-amd64 root=/dev/nvme0n1p3 rw quiet splash resume=UUID=1fdb4317-7925-4369-8060-933dbf2f6f34

# 2. Load the initrd
initrd /boot/initrd.img-6.10.11-amd64

# 3. Boot
boot
```

